Related Rates With Rectangular Solids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "73d951b2-ddb7-5e7a-805a-d3f9c5c7afd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1061 897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33231a76-9fca-526a-a863-28a8b9ba5ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1061 898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de101021-b5be-55c7-a7f9-401670121e58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1061 901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eecc2511-6d16-5fc5-877c-1bd58b5fd752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1061 904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fcc1570-0b7e-5714-8da4-85c43cb9a511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to solve related rates problems in the context of squares. We can use the same methods to solve related rates problems in the context of cubes, too.\n\nIn general, to solve a related rates problem, we need to follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n**Step 2:** Write down a relation between the time-dependent variables.\n\n**Step 3:** Differentiate the relation with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nLet's see this in action in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1065 484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff60fc65-18a2-5e55-a0e1-0ecbd0698507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500498 #edb/ref 1080 "42\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500498 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d1b4802-4fbf-597a-8717-edd9da5ff601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500528 #edb/ref 1080 "43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500528 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500528 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d66b81f-aab7-5dee-bc76-93149d565137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500678 #edb/ref 1080 "44\\,100{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "651e5094-ed0f-5287-afa0-88760a288cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500700 #edb/ref 1080 "40\\,150{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70ef183c-3e32-589d-bdef-3a17eb486140" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500876 #edb/ref 1080 "41\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c387d300-dcb3-5156-be2d-bccac09b58bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501066 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7049e9d7-8f84-594a-912b-f5fcab69cbfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ea/ea7c818a6694ba959c5230dcde8611a7b1dc44bcad383f54c93d2934336c35b6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $9\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $40\\text{ cm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d8efcf9-a827-5e33-8982-8c950b1390fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=40\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=40$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(40)^{2}\\cdot (-9)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-43\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$ at the moment when $x=40\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
